# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# GitHub Pages ignores this file (Jekyll skips underscore-prefixed paths), so it
# is safe to keep in the repo alongside the pages it serves.
#
# There is deliberately NO Content-Security-Policy here. Every page carries
# large inline <script> blocks and inline event handlers, and a CSP strict
# enough to be worth having would break booking, the gallery and the pay page
# all at once. It should ship as its own tested change, not as a side effect of
# moving domains.
#
# Verified before adding: no page uses an iframe, getUserMedia, the camera, the
# microphone or geolocation, so none of these restrictions can bite.
#
# ── Cache-Control, and why it is here at all ───────────────────────────────────
#
# Cloudflare Pages was serving this site's assets with `max-age=14400` — four
# hours — from URLs that never change. That is fine for a file whose bytes never
# change, and wrong for every file on a site that gets edited: after a deploy, a
# visitor's browser kept its copy of assistant.js WITHOUT ASKING, while the HTML
# beside it (already `max-age=0, must-revalidate`) refetched and updated. The
# result was a page from today running yesterday's widget, and it looked like a
# refresh bug: one browser showed the change and another did not, because the
# difference was never the browser — it was whether that browser happened to have
# the old file cached. `must-revalidate` alone does not save you: it only binds
# AFTER the copy goes stale, and it is not stale for four hours.
#
# So the rule is stated once, for the whole site, rather than asset by asset: a
# list is a list somebody forgets to add to, and the day they forget is the day a
# client sees a half-updated page. Every response here revalidates. The cost is
# one conditional request per file per visit, answered as a 304 when nothing has
# changed — cheap, parallel, and always correct.
#
# It is deliberately in THIS block rather than a more specific one: Cloudflare
# merges the values of a header set by two rules that both match a path, so a
# `/*` rule and an `/asset.js` rule both setting Cache-Control produce one header
# with both values in it, and the first `max-age` wins. One rule, one value.
/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
